-- Lesson 12 as the first interactive lesson (Amit, 3 Nov 2026). Needs members v98 and the voice file in R2.
update lesson_guides set data = $g${"title": "Limits and an off switch", "kind": "Lesson 12", "mins": 5, "steps": [{"t": "Safety comes first", "d": "Day twelve. Limits and an off switch. Quick one first: this voice is AI. The words are mine. Strategy rules decide what to buy. Safety rules decide what the robot is never allowed to do, and they run first. Two of them work on every order. No single holding goes above a set share of your account: fifteen percent by default. And before any order, it checks the prices are fresh, nothing is already pending, and it only touches what it owns.", "lx": {"from": 0.0, "to": 30.3, "try": {"type": "tiles", "title": "Tap each limit", "items": [["Holding cap", "No single holding above 15% of your account."], ["Checks before any order", "Fresh prices, nothing already pending, and only what the robot owns."], ["Safety stop", "Next step. It's the one people ask about most."]]}}}, {"t": "The safety stop", "d": "The third limit is the safety stop. Your robot remembers your account's highest value. If the account falls fifteen percent from that high on Balanced, or twenty on Adventurous, it stops changing anything. No buying. No selling. There's no button to press. It checks every week, and carries on by itself once you're back above the line. Try it: drag the slider and watch what happens.", "lx": {"from": 30.3, "to": 54.56, "try": {"type": "line", "title": "Move the market", "intro": "Example account on Balanced. Its high was £5,000, so the line is £4,250.", "high": 5000, "line": 4250}}}, {"t": "Your off switch", "d": "You also have an off switch, and it's in Robot Dash. Press Pause my robot, confirm with Face ID, and nothing new is proposed or sent. It doesn't sell anything. What you hold stays as it is, and still moves with the market. If you want out of a holding, you do that yourself in Trading 212. Restart it the same way. Try it now. This one's pretend.", "lx": {"from": 54.56, "to": 77.61, "try": {"type": "pause", "note": "Before real money, pause and restart once in your own Robot Dash: the go-live check looks for it."}}}, {"t": "When to pause", "d": "So when do you use it? Only when something looks broken: an order refused and you don't know why, or a number in your email that looks wrong. Not because the market is down. Not because of the news. And not for a holiday: your plan comes to your phone, one tap approves it, and if you miss a week, nothing happens. Got a worry? Tap one, and see what the robot says.", "lx": {"from": 77.61, "to": 99.64, "try": {"type": "ask", "title": "Ask your robot", "items": [["The market's down 5% this week. Should I pause you?", "No. Falling markets are what my rules are for. I'll run my weekly check as normal."], ["There's a scary headline about something I hold.", "I don't read the news, on purpose. My rules decide, not the headlines."], ["I'm on holiday for two weeks.", "Enjoy it. One tap approves my plan from your phone. Miss a week and nothing happens."], ["An order was refused and I don't know why.", "That's when to pause me. Then read the reason, and Robot Care can look at it with you."]]}}}, {"t": "We took the L", "d": "Two Ls we took here. On our first live day, one order bounced: the plan asked for more cash than was really there. So your limits must match the money in the account. And we were sure the safety stop was a mistake, so we tested it on six years of prices, with the pass mark written down first. Nothing we tried beat it. But the account got back above the line every time. In an invented fall lasting a whole year, it did far worse. So it stays, as a rule, not a promise. Capital at risk. I'm an engineer, not a financial adviser.", "lx": {"from": 99.64, "to": 135.69, "try": {"type": "points", "title": "We took the L", "items": ["Limits must match the real money", "The stop stays, as a rule, not a promise"]}}}], "tips": ["Room before the safety stop is on your app's Overview, so you can glance at it without opening the email.", "The off switch is for when something looks broken. Not falling markets, and not holidays.", "Pausing stops new orders. It never sells anything.", "The safety stop is different. It lifts by itself once your account is back above the line, and there's no restart button."]}$g$::jsonb, updated_at = now() where id = 'day-12';
